---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import manifest from "../../../languages.json";

// Single source for the repository URL. Must match the actual remote
// (alleato-llc/gota) — every "view the source" link on the page derives from it.
const REPO = "https://github.com/alleato-llc/gota";
const BLOB = `${REPO}/blob/main`;

// The language templates come from the repo-root languages.json — the ONE list.
// Adding a language means editing that file; this page needs no change. A unit test
// (tests/test_languages.py) fails if the manifest and the rest of the repo disagree.
const languages = manifest.languages;

// Languages as rows: the harness file you copy, the seam you write, and the
// build-and-run command — all from the same manifest. Python is tinted because it
// also hosts the orchestrator (harness.py + run.py).
const cmpCols = ["Harness (copy as-is)", "Your op (one seam)", "Build & run"];
const cmpRows = manifest.languages.map((l) => ({
  name: l.name,
  ref: l.orchestrator === true,
  cells: [l.harness, l.seam, l.build],
}));

// Where gota sits among performance approaches. The micro-benchmark row is tinted
// because that is gota's level; the others answer different questions. This is a
// definition, not a comparison: no tools are named, gota's or anyone else's.
const approachCols = ["What it measures", "The question it answers"];
const approachRows = [
  {
    name: "Micro-benchmark",
    ref: true,
    cells: [
      "One small operation, in isolation, looped",
      "How fast is this specific code path?",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Macro / application",
    cells: [
      "A whole program under a realistic workload",
      "How fast is the system, end to end?",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Profiling",
    cells: [
      "A real run, sampled to find hot spots",
      "Where is the time actually spent?",
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="gota — cross-language throughput micro-benchmarks"
  description="A small, copy-it reference for honest cross-language throughput micro-benchmarks: a protocol, a generic Python orchestrator, per-language runner templates in ten languages, and a self-contained HTML report."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Educational · Polyglot · Copy, don't depend</span>
      <h1>gota<span class="kanji">・滴</span></h1>
      <p class="tagline">Cross-language throughput benchmarks, drop by drop.</p>
      <p class="lead">
        The same operation, implemented in ten languages, measured the same
        way so the numbers actually compare. gota is a measurement protocol, a
        generic Python orchestrator, a runner template per language, and a
        self-contained HTML report. Copy it into your project; the protocol is
        the product, the code is small enough to re-type.
      </p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn primary" href={REPO}>View the source</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href={`${BLOB}/PROTOCOL.md`}>Read the protocol</a>
      </div>
      <p class="fine">
        Ten languages · one JSON line per benchmark · no fabricated numbers
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="micro">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What a micro-benchmark is</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        A micro-benchmark isolates one small operation — a hash, a parse, a
        buffer copy — and runs it in a tight loop to measure how fast that single
        code path goes. It deliberately leaves out everything around it: no
        network, no disk, no real workload. That isolation is the whole point. It
        is what makes one operation's speed a clean, comparable number — here, the
        same operation across ten languages.
      </p>
      <div class="cmp-scroll">
        <table class="cmp">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Approach</th>
              {approachCols.map((c) => <th scope="col">{c}</th>)}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              approachRows.map((row) => (
                <tr class={row.ref ? "cmp-ref" : undefined}>
                  <th scope="row">{row.name}</th>
                  {row.cells.map((c) => (
                    <td>{c}</td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="sub" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
        These answer different questions and are complements, not rivals: a
        micro-benchmark tells you which implementation of an operation is faster,
        a macro benchmark tells you whether the whole system is fast enough, and a
        profiler tells you where to look. gota lives in the first row.
      </p>
      <p class="sub" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
        gota lives in the first row, with one distinguishing property: the same
        operation is measured the same way in ten languages, so the results land in
        one table and compare <em>across</em> languages. What you copy is a ~20-line
        protocol rather than a package you install, which is what lets every runner
        stay native to its own language.
      </p>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <b>One more axis: throughput, not latency.</b> Even within
        micro-benchmarking, gota measures <em>throughput</em> — the peak rate
        (MB/s) a loop sustains, the unimpeded speed, which is what you want for
        comparing implementations. Per-call latency and its distribution (mean, p99,
        outlier rejection) is a different measurement, and batching deliberately
        discards the per-call overhead it would need. gota answers a narrower
        question on purpose, and the <a href="#protocol">protocol</a> below is how
        it keeps that one answer honest and comparable.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="layers">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Three layers, one contract</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        A runner is usable on its own; the orchestrator is just the conductor
        that runs them together; the report is a pure consumer of the JSON. You
        can stop at any layer. Between the first two sits the protocol every
        runner obeys.
      </p>
      <div class="layers">
        <div class="layer">
          <div>
            <b>Measure.</b>
            <span>
              {" "}One native runner per language. Copy <code>gota.&lt;lang&gt;</code>
              as-is; it runs the peak-of-batches timing loop and prints one JSON
              line, <code>{`{"impl","bench","mbps","mbps_median","iters"}`}</code>, per benchmark.
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <div>
            <b>Orchestrate.</b>
            <span>
              {" "}Python, once, across every language. <code>harness.py</code>
              builds and runs each runner under identical parameters, collects
              their JSON, records machine, date, git, and toolchain-version
              provenance, and writes <code>results.json</code> + a Markdown table.
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <div>
            <b>Report.</b>
            <span>
              {" "}Optional and generic over the format. <code>report.py</code>
              turns any <code>results.json</code> into a standalone, sortable HTML
              viewer with a file picker, metric-aware units (MB/s or ops/sec), and
              baseline comparison.
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="languages">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Ten languages, one protocol</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        Two files per language: <code>gota.*</code> is the harness you copy
        untouched; <code>runner.*</code> is your code, where one seam
        (<code>run(impl, register)</code> then <code>bench(name, op)</code>) plugs
        an operation into the timing loop. Each runner is independently runnable.
      </p>
      <div class="grid">
        {
          languages.map((l) => (
            <div class="card">
              <h3>{l.name}</h3>
              <p>{l.body}</p>
              <p class="card-files">{l.files}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="cmp-scroll" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <table class="cmp">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Language</th>
              {cmpCols.map((c) => <th scope="col">{c}</th>)}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              cmpRows.map((row) => (
                <tr class={row.ref ? "cmp-ref" : undefined}>
                  <th scope="row">{row.name}</th>
                  {row.cells.map((c) => (
                    <td><code>{c}</code></td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="sub" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        Python is tinted because it also hosts the orchestrator
        (<code>harness.py</code> + your <code>run.py</code>). Every other row is
        just a runner. Full build notes live in each
        <a href={`${BLOB}/templates`}><code>templates/&lt;lang&gt;/README.md</code></a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="report">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>See a live report</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        The bundled example — FNV-1a across all ten languages — rendered by
        <code>report.py</code> into a standalone, sortable HTML viewer. One toggle in
        the report flips the same measurement between byte throughput (MB/s) and
        operations per second (one op = hashing the whole 64 KiB buffer): same numbers,
        same ranking, two units.
      </p>
      <div class="cta" style="justify-content: flex-start;">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/report.html">Open the live report</a>
      </div>
      <p class="sub" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        A self-contained page with a file picker, so you can drop any other
        <code>results.json</code> in and re-render. Generated from the committed example
        data, never hand-edited.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="use">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What you can do with the output</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        Nothing here is a closed pipeline. Each layer hands you a plain artifact, so you
        can stop at the one you need and route the rest into your own systems.
      </p>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li>
          <b>Just the numbers.</b> <code>run_all()</code> returns a plain list of dicts,
          one per benchmark, with a per-runner <code>timeout=</code> so a hung runner is
          killed and skipped instead of stalling the whole run.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Compare against a baseline.</b> Point the report at two or more
          <code>results.json</code> files for a per-cell delta table. A change inside the
          noise band (±2% by default) is not flagged, and comparing across different
          machines or parameters is called out as a warning rather than shown as a
          meaningful delta.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Gate CI on it.</b> <code>--fail-on-regression PCT</code> exits non-zero when
          any cell drops more than <code>PCT</code>% below baseline;
          <code>--markdown</code> emits the same comparison as a table for a PR comment.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Send it anywhere.</b> Outputs accept a path <em>or</em> any writable stream,
          so results can go to a file, to stdout, into memory, or straight to an HTTP
          body without touching disk.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Measure something other than bytes.</b> A run records the <em>kind</em> of
          quantity it measured — byte throughput, operation throughput, latency — apart
          from the free-text unit label, so the report headlines the right number and a
          comparison refuses to pit different kinds against each other.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="protocol">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The protocol is the product</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        Every runner obeys the same recipe, so numbers compare even though each
        runner is native to its language. This is the same discipline a
        multi-port project uses to keep ports in sync; here the ports are the
        timing harnesses.
      </p>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li>
          <b>Same three CLI args</b> — buffer bytes, warmup seconds, measure
          seconds — passed identically to every runner.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Peak of batches, with a stability check.</b> Warm up the JIT, grow a
          batch until it runs &ge; 100ms, then report the fastest batch's MB/s
          next to the median of all batches — a close gap means a clean run, a wide
          one means the peak is noisy. The clock is read only at batch boundaries,
          never around one tiny op.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>One JSON line per benchmark</b> on stdout
          (<code>{`{"impl","bench","mbps","mbps_median","iters"}`}</code>) — the
          entire wire format between a runner and the orchestrator.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Kept equivalent across all ten templates.</b> Change the loop in
          one and you change it in all of them, and in
          <code>PROTOCOL.md</code>, or they stop being comparable.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="sub" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
        The contract carries its own version — currently <b>protocol 1.1.0</b> — so a
        project that copied its runners can say which version they implement, and a copy
        that has fallen behind is a fact you can check rather than a guess.
      </p>
      <div class="cta" style="justify-content: flex-start; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <a class="btn ghost" href={`${BLOB}/PROTOCOL.md`}>Read the spec</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href={`${BLOB}/docs/DESIGN.md`}>Why it is shaped this way</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="hazard">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The easiest way to measure nothing</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        The classic micro-benchmark trap: if nothing observes your operation's
        result, an optimizing compiler is free to delete the whole computation — and
        you measure nanoseconds of nothing, a confident wrong number. gota's templates
        guard against it in every language.
      </p>
      <div class="layers">
        <div class="layer">
          <div>
            <b>Compiled languages — dead-code elimination.</b>
            <span>
              {" "}In any optimizing compiler a result that is never read can be deleted
              outright, so the loop measures an empty loop at full speed. Every runner
              consumes its op's result through a one-byte sink, so the work has to
              survive.
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <div>
            <b>Haskell — laziness.</b>
            <span>
              {" "}The sharpest case: in a lazy language an unforced computation is a
              thunk that never runs, and an invariant one is computed once and shared.
              The harness threads each result into the next call and forces it, so the
              work can be neither deferred nor shared. Same hazard, made a language
              requirement.
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <div>
            <b>Why it matters here.</b>
            <span>
              {" "}Peak-of-batches is only honest if the batches did real work. The sink
              discipline is what keeps the number a measurement instead of a fiction — the
              same reason every <code>runner.*</code> carries a sink and Haskell's seam is
              shaped the way it is.
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="honest">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Honest by design</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        gota reports peak (unimpeded) throughput on a normal machine, which is
        right for <em>comparing</em> implementations and optimistic for
        real-world latency. It says so, and it never invents a number.
      </p>
      <div class="note">
        <b>What the numbers mean.</b> Peak-of-batches is the unimpeded rate, not
        a latency figure, and naive code shows language/runtime overhead, not how
        fast tuned or SIMD code can go. Every figure comes from a real run on a
        stated machine; <code>RESULTS.md</code>, <code>results.json</code>, and
        <code>report.html</code> are generated, never hand-edited, and CI numbers
        (whose hardware varies) are never committed.
      </div>
      <p class="sub" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        And it is deliberately narrow: it reports sustained throughput, not
        nanosecond latency distributions, and not lab-grade absolute numbers — those
        would want pinned cores and a locked clock on a dedicated box. What it gives
        you is a comparable picture, drop by drop.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="source">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Copy and run</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        Copy <code>harness.py</code> and the <code>templates/&lt;lang&gt;</code>
        for each language you benchmark, swap your operation into each
        <code>runner.*</code>, write a small <code>run.py</code>, and run it. The
        bundled example ties all ten languages together end to end.
      </p>
      <pre class="snippet mono"><code><span class="cm"># run one language's runner standalone (prints one JSON line)</span>
python3 templates/python/runner.py 65536 0.2 0.4

<span class="cm"># run the full example: build + run every runner, then tabulate</span>
python3 examples/run.py            <span class="cm"># writes results.json + RESULTS.md</span>
python3 report.py examples/results.json -o examples/report.html</code></pre>

      <div class="cta" style="justify-content: flex-start; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <a class="btn primary" href={REPO}>Browse the code</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href={`${BLOB}/examples`}>See the example</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
